Description: PGP certificates that say whose they are
 The PGP ID model gives a PGP certificate a decentralised entity
 identifier: who this key belongs to, said once and checked by people rather
 than by an authority.
 .
 pgpid reads and changes certificates through that model — identifiers,
 validity, ownertrust, addresses, vCard properties, avatars — vouches for
 other people, drives a PGP security key, and puts a secret key on paper
 as QR codes so that any three of five fragments rebuild it.
 .
 What it depends on is what one of its actions spawns: gnupg for every one of
 them and scdaemon for the card half, qrencode and zbar-tools for the paper in
 both directions, libgfshare-bin for the sharing itself, librsvg2-bin to draw
 both sheets — the fragments and the business card — the cups pair to ask
 which printers there are and to send to one, graphicsmagick to fit a picture
 into a certificate and to read a scanned PDF, and passwd for the local
 accounts. The bash libraries are for the two shell programs beside the
 compiled tool: they source them at their first line and do not start without.
 .
 Only sudo is optional. Without it the system actions still run, but only for
 root, and there is then no SUDO_USER whose keyring and trust they can read —
 use their --from option to name one.
 .
 The two shell programs are pgpid-gen, which generates a certificate from a
 passport, and pgpid-qrscan, which moves secrets onto a smartcard. Reading a
 passport is what facedetect and tesseract-ocr are for, and pgpid-gen asks
 gpg-wks-client whether an address supports WKD.
